.TH NIGHTCORE 1 "2026-10-02" "nightcore" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
nightcore \- speed up or pitch-shift audio, losslessly
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B nightcore
[\fIOPTIONS\fR] [\fIinput\fR]
.br
.B nightcore --pitch-diff
\fIF\fR [\fIF\fR]
.br
.B nightcore --speed-diff
\fIST\fR
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBnightcore\fR is a simple wrapper that creates bit-perfect Nightcore by altering the
sample rate of the audio to change speed/pitch file using FFmpeg.
.PP
It can also independently pitch audio with rubberband. Shifting up
first upsamples with SoX (shifted highs would otherwise fold back as
audible garbage); shifting down pitches at the current rate and settles
the rate afterwards (pre-downsampling would brickwall restorable highs).
Either way duration is preserved. Resampling that would clip a hot
master is redone with headroom instead of flat-topping it, and integer
encodes of processed audio use triangular dither.
.PP
You can also encode music in a standard sample rate like (ex: 48kHz, 88.2kHz, 96kHz...)
automatically to be chosen to be the smallest standard frequency that's still
higher than the frequency that FFmpeg's `asetrate` changed.
.PP
Most of the flags here should cover 99% of all nightcore tracks from the 2000s.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B -s, --speed \fISPEED\fR
Speed factor (1.2 = 20% faster). Default 1.0. Given an \fB.lrc\fR lyrics
file instead of audio, every timestamp (and the \fI[length:]\fR header)
is scaled by 1/\fISPEED\fR; the lyrics mirror the parent audio's output
name (same stem, \fB.lrc\fR extension), falling back to \fIsong
[1.2x].lrc\fR without a same-stem audio file. At speed 1 the lyrics
are copied byte-identical under the mirrored name. Only \fB-s\fR and
\fB-r\fR change lyrics; the rest only shape the mirrored name.
.TP
.B -r, --reciprocal
Use 1/speed instead (undo a speedup).
.TP
.B -y, --overwrite
Overwrite existing output files.
.TP
.B -q, --quiet
Drop tool output but keep stage/progress lines. Repeat as
\fB-qq\fR for warnings and errors only; a failed tool's captured
output always prints.
.TP
.B -R, --resample
Resample to next standard rate via SoX (lossless output only).
-R means standard-rate-only output to
\fI./std-samplerate/[format]/\fR in batch mode (single-file runs
resample in place, in the current directory); no weird-rate copy is kept. Default keeps the non-standard rate,
bit-perfect, in the current directory. Cannot be used with lossy output
(lossy encoders manage rates themselves; resample a lossless
intermediate first).
.TP
.B -F, --format \fIFMT\fR
Output format: \fBflac\fR (default), \fBalac\fR, \fBmp3\fR,
\fBopus\fR, \fBaac\fR (.m4a); \fBmkv\fR (default), \fBmp4\fR or
\fBmp4alac\fR with
\fB--mkvid\fR (which picks its own container).
Automatic quality defaults exist only for lossless output, tuned for most
compression (FLAC level 12; ALAC has no knobs). Lossy outputs prefer
the best encoder -- MP3 via lame CLI (libmp3lame fallback), Opus via
opusenc (ffmpeg libopus fallback), AAC via qaac (ffmpeg AAC fallback with
a quality warning) -- with quality defaults V0 (mp3), 128k (opus)
and TVBR 91 (qaac); ffmpeg AAC has none and fails loudly unless explicit
\fB--lossy-opts\fR are passed. User opts replace defaults wholesale.
Rates always pass through untouched (\fB-R\fR with lossy output
is an error): if a weird rate is unacceptable the encoder itself
fails loudly. Lossy files carry only the shift tag in filename and Title:
no source-quality or rate tags. With a genuine \fBqaac\fR on PATH (verified via
.IR "qaac --check" ,
even under wine), ALAC/AAC encode with CoreAudio; otherwise ffmpeg's
encoders are used.
.TP
.B -p, --pitch
Pitch-shift only, keep duration (needs rubberband; SoX upsamples first
so shifted highs survive). Labelled semitones rounded to 2 decimals,
e.g.
.IR "[3.16st]" ;
exact values live in the pipeline tag, which \fI-D\fR inverts exactly.
.TP
.B -S, --semitones \fIST\fR
Pitch-shift by semitones: alone it implies \fB-p\fR; with
\fB-s\fR it makes a combined speed+pitch job.
.TP
.B -n, --dry-run
Print output filename(s), convert nothing.
.TP
.B --autorename \fR[\fITEMPLATE\fR]
Rename output via a filename-stem template with bare \fI{artist} {title}
{diff} {quality} {resample} {denightcore}\fR slots (with \fI{title}\fR
always the source title -- a tag-template \fItitle=\fR shapes the Title
tag only, never filenames; \fI{diff}\fR is full precision,
\fI{diff(N)}\fR rounds as in tag templates). No flag keeps the input
filename and appends params; a bare \fI--autorename\fR flag renders
the default template
\fI"{artist} - {title} [Nightcore {diff}] [{quality}] [{resample}]
[{denightcore}]"\fR (empty slots drop their brackets; lossy outputs
drop quality/rate). \fI--autorename=\fR with an explicit empty value
keeps the legacy \fIArtist - Title [tags]\fR (base
cleaned of script groups, suffix recovered from filename tags under
\fI-N\fR, so re-runs are idempotent). A bare flag goes last or
uses \fI=\fR, else it eats the input filename as its template.
.TP
.B -o, --output \fIFILE\fR
Override the output filename (extension required, e.g. \fI-o mix.mp3\fR;
used verbatim). Single-file runs only: \fI--batch\fR + \fI-o\fR is
an error.
.TP
.B --output-folder \fIDIR\fR
Base for batch folders (\fIencodes/denightcore/renametag/std-samplerate\fR,
each with a \fI[format]/\fR subfolder except where noted).
An existing \fIDIR\fR nests them (\fIDIR/encodes/[format]/...); a missing
\fIDIR\fR takes the folder's place (\fIDIR/[format]/...). Single-file
runs redirect their output into \fIDIR\fR instead.
.TP
.B --in-place
With \fI-N\fR: renametag alongside the inputs instead of
copying to \fI./renametag/\fR. Asks \fI[y/N]\fR first (default no):
originals move. Needs \fI-N\fR; cannot be combined with
\fI--output-folder\fR or \fI-o\fR.
.TP
.B -N, --renametag
Renametag mode: rewrite tags and rename via stream copy, with no audio
processing. Each file keeps its container (\fI-F\fR is ignored).
Copies land flat in \fI./renametag/\fR (originals stay); \fI--in-place\fR
moves alongside the inputs instead (asks \fI[y/N]\fR first),
\fI-o\fR names the copy verbatim.
Same-stem .lrc lyrics follow their audio master (renamed or copied
alongside it); batch .lrc entries are skipped as sidecar-driven.
Names come from \fI--autorename\fR (default template) with no
conversion params appended beyond its slots. Speed/pitch/resample flags
(\fI-s -S -p -r -R -D\fR) are errors; a same-name
rewrite goes through a temp file plus atomic replace. Opus pictures
cannot be preserved without re-encoding, so those fail loudly.
A stamp that already carries a \fISettings:\fR line is preserved as-is;
otherwise the shift is recovered from filename tags when present (old
stamp-less files gain a real stamp and become \fI-D\fR-able). The
recovered shift also feeds the \fI{diff}\fR slot of template titles,
so retags print the same \fITitle [Nightcore diff]\fR style as fresh
encodes.
.TP
.B --no-delete-tags
With \fI--tag-template\fR: keep source tags not listed in the template
(default: strip them, template only). Needs a template.
.TP
.B --no-stamp
Skip the \fIMade with nightcore.py\fR DESCRIPTION provenance tag
(default: every output carries it, overriding any template description;
mapped source copies are scrubbed too).
.TP
.B --no-pipeline
Skip the encoder_settings pipeline tag: shift, resample target, output
format, source fingerprint and the versions of the tools that ran
(qaac encodes also record the CoreAudioToolbox version doing the
actual work; opusenc-style; what \fI-D\fR reads back). Default writes
it (mapped source copies are scrubbed too). \fI--no-stamp\fR and
\fI--no-pipeline\fR are independent: drop the branding for anonymous
posts, keep the data that makes reversals work. Template-explicit
values still apply either way. FLAC outputs needing tag fixes require
metaflac.
.TP
.B --tag-template \fIFILE\fR
Apply a tag template file: one \fIkey="value"\fR entry per tag (values
may span lines). Empty or bare values keep the source tag; tags missing
from the file are stripped from outputs unless \fI--no-delete-tags\fR
is given. \fItitle=\fR and \fIartist=\fR values may use bare
\fI{title} {artist} {diff} {quality} {resample} {denightcore}\fR slots
(brackets are yours; \fI{title}\fR/\fI{artist}\fR mean the source tags;
\fI{diff(N)}\fR rounds the shift for display, e.g. \fI{diff(2)}\fR),
e.g. \fItitle="{title} [Nightcore {diff(2)}]"\fR. An explicit
\fItitle=\fR is used verbatim for every file; without one, titles
default to \fITitle [Nightcore diff]\fR (rounded to 2 decimals, full
precision stays in the pipeline tag). Template tag names are
case-insensitive and stored lowercase; \fIalbumartist=\fR is stored
canonically except on m4a (which needs the \fIalbum_artist\fR spelling).
All other
values stay literal. Template \fIcomment\fR lands in COMMENT (ffmpeg alone
would file it as DESCRIPTION) and \fIencoder\fR stubs are honored on
FLAC; muxer-written encoder tags are stripped by default (lossy outputs
without a template keep the encoder's own tags instead).
.TP
.B --cover \fIIMG\fR
PNG/JPG artwork to use as the sole attached picture of outputs
(opus pictures go via opusenc).
.TP
.B --mkvid
Remux one song (plus one optional \fI.lrc\fR lyric and an image) into a
1080p YouTube video: no audio processing, just a remux. The image is
letterboxed/pillarboxed into 1920x1080 with black bars (never stretched)
and sits under a \fClibx264\fR (\fIpreset veryfast, tune stillimage,
crf 18\fR) still picture. The audio rides as FLAC in \fBmkv\fR, AAC in
\fBmp4\fR (default, qaac TVBR 91 unless \fB--lossy-opts\fR overrides)
or ALAC in \fBmp4alac\fR -- \fBmp4\fR plays everywhere (Discord and
friends reject ALAC, mkv support is even worse) while \fBmkv\fR and
\fBmp4alac\fR stay lossless, so YouTube's Opus transcode is exactly one
generation of loss; no resampling happens (mkv/mp4alac + FLAC/ALAC accept
any sample rate; an AAC encoder choking on a weird rate fails loudly).
\fB-F\fR picks \fBmkv\fR (default), \fBmp4\fR or \fBmp4alac\fR.
Without \fB--cover\fR the song's embedded cover art is used; a song with
no image and no \fB--cover\fR fails loudly. \fI.lrc\fR lyrics are
converted to SubRip and embedded as a subtitle track (\fBsrt\fR in mkv,
\fBmov_text\fR in mp4). All audio tags (title, artist, album, date,
genre ...) are carried into the video, ready for YouTube. Audio
processing flags (\fB-s\fR, \fB-S\fR, \fB-p\fR, \fB-r\fR, \fB-R\fR,
\fB-D\fR), \fB--batch\fR, \fB--renametag\fR and \fB--in-place\fR are
refused; \fB--quiet\fR, \fB--autorename\fR and \fB--tag-template\fR are
silently ignored.
.TP
.B --version
Print version and exit (appends +g<hash> when installed from git).
.TP
.B --check
Check every external tool (ffmpeg/ffprobe, SoX, rubberband, lame,
opusenc, qaac, metaflac, opusinfo, vorbiscomment), the ffmpeg-bundled
encoders and the python mutagen library; print one
\fIok <version> (<path>)\fR or \fIMISSING (<hint>)\fR line per tool,
per-format encode readiness, and a verdict. Exits 0 when all required
tools are present, 1 otherwise (preferred-but-optional encoders and
tag readers never fail the run). Same output on Linux and the Windows
builds (which carry their tools in \fItools\\\fR next to the exe).
.TP
.B --lossy-opts \fIOPTS\fR
Encoder quality flags (shlex-split, equals form only). Defaults: V0
(mp3), 128k (opus), TVBR 91 (qaac); ffmpeg AAC has none and fails loudly
unless explicit opts are passed (it isn't that great: install qaac).
Your opts replace the defaults wholesale. The flag
dialect picks the encoder: native-CLI style (\fI-V\fR, \fI--bitrate\fR,
any \fI--long-opt\fR) uses lame/opusenc/qaac, \fI-X:a\fR style uses the
ffmpeg encoder; mixing both is an error. A forced-but-missing encoder
fails loudly instead of falling back. Ignored (with a warning) for
lossless outputs (including --mkvid mkv/mp4alac); --mkvid mp4 shares
the AAC dialect and TVBR 91 default. Only the equals form is accepted: a space-separated
value starting with \fB-\fR would parse as a nightcore flag, so it is
rejected outright.
.TP
.B --batch \fR[\fIDIR\fR]
Convert every audio file in \fIDIR\fR (default: .) instead of one input;
outputs go to \fI./encodes/[format]/\fR (relative to the current working
directory; see \fI--output-folder\fR). \fI-R\fR outputs (standard rate
only) go to \fI./std-samplerate/[format]/\fR instead, \fI-D\fR batches
to \fI./denightcore/[format]/\fR (lossless output enforced), and \fI-N\fR
batches copy to flat \fI./renametag/\fR. Cover art
found next to the inputs is copied alongside the audio. Folders left empty
are removed. Tag-derived names can collide there (duplicate files, or
lossy outputs with stripped quality tags): the first file keeps its
name, later ones get \fI" (2)"\fR, \fI" (3)"\fR, ... appended.
Reruns skip existing outputs (add files freely); \fB-y\fR re-encodes
everything from scratch. Cover art found next to the inputs
(\fIcover.png\fR, \fIfolder.jpg\fR, ...) is copied alongside the
outputs (same skip rules).
\fB.lrc\fR lyrics files mirror their parent audio's output name
(same stem, \fB.lrc\fR extension).
.TP
.B --stats \fR[\fIDIR\fR]
Print statistics for every audio file in \fIDIR\fR (default: .) at depth
1 (subdirectories are not entered) and exit: how many audio files have
same-stem \fB.lrc\fR lyrics, average/median speed and the speed
distribution, shift source (pipeline stamp vs filename tags vs
unstamped), pitch shifts, truly-lossless vs lossy-origin vs lossy
counts, containers, source codecs, average bitrates, depth, sample
rates. Speeds read the trusted pipeline tag first,
falling back to filename tags. Nothing is converted, so every other
flag except \fI--recurse\fR is ignored. The speed distribution draws
block bars next to the counts. The report ends with a \fIGenerated by
nightcore.py @ date\fR stamp, so it can be redirected to a file.
.TP
.B --recurse \fR[\fIN\fR]
With \fI--stats\fR: descend into subdirectories (for Artist/Album
packs). The bare flag scans everything; \fI--recurse N\fR caps at
\fIN\fR levels (the folder itself is level 1, so \fI--recurse 2\fR
adds one sublevel). Lyrics pair per directory: a same-stem
\fB.lrc\fR only counts for audio next to it. Needs \fI--stats\fR.
.TP
.B --pitch-diff \fIF\fR [\fIF\fR]
Calculator, no conversion. One value = speed factor; two values = peak
frequencies (original, pitched). Prints semitones, cents, speed factor.
.TP
.B -D, --denightcore
Undo from the pipeline tag only (encoder_settings): inverts the
recorded shift. Records without the \fINiGhTcOrE\fR magic marker are
refused, as are filenames alone, so a file with
neither record fails (batch runs skip untagged files with a tally
instead). Pre-magic files gain the marker through a \fI-N\fR round
(old \fISettings:\fR lines migrate too); files with only filename
tags need \fI-s/-r\fR manually if no record exists at all, and WAV
inputs carry no readable tags, so they can never be undone from tags.
Names rebuild from tags through the \fI--autorename\fR template
(denightcore verdict via \fI{denightcore}\fR, restored quality via
\fI{quality}\fR, plus a resample tag when resampling
now; artist prefix omitted without an
artist tag).
\fI--tag-template\fR is ignored (restoring, not curating). The output
stamp records the undo, so a second \fI-D\fR on it refuses (nothing left
to invert). Output must be lossless: lossy \fI-F\fR is rejected, so tracks
never lose quality over time. Batch outputs land in
\fI./denightcore/[format]/\fR. Bit-perfect needs a pure sample-rate
inversion -- no pitch resynthesis, no resampling now or in the forward
job, lossless in and out; anything else reports NBP with reasons.
Nightcore -> denightcore cycles of pure speedups are bit-exact and
stable; pitch/resample jobs lose a generation per cycle.
.TP
.B --speed-diff \fIST\fR
Calculator, no conversion: semitones to speed factor.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Speed up 20%:
.PP
.RS
nightcore song.flac -s 1.2 -y
.RE
.PP
Same as a player-safe resampled copy:
.PP
.RS
nightcore song.flac -s 1.2 -R -y
.RE
.PP
Pitch-only remake from measured semitones:
.PP
.RS
nightcore --pitch-diff 54 63
nightcore song.flac -S 2.6687090560373763 -y
.RE
.PP
Sped up and pitched up in one run:
.PP
.RS
nightcore song.flac -s 1.2 -S 2.166866948 -y
.RE
.PP
Undo a 1.2x speedup:
.PP
.RS
nightcore "Song [1.2x].flac" -s 1.2 -r -y
.RE
.PP
Undo from filename tags:
.PP
.RS
nightcore "Song [1.2x].flac" -D -y
.RE
.PP
Retime lyrics for the sped-up audio:
.PP
.RS
nightcore song.lrc -s 1.2 -y
.RE
.PP
Preview names for a bulk rename:
.PP
.RS
for f in *.flac; do nightcore "$f" -s 1.2 -n; done
.RE
